<h2>Freight economics drive this trade</h2>
<p>Where India supplies African markets, it's typically because Indian origin offers a freight or price advantage over more distant competing suppliers — soybean meal into Kenya is a clear example, alongside rice, spices and organic manure moving to various East and West African destinations.</p>

<h2>Our published data coverage here is thinner than other regions</h2>
<p>We've been direct throughout this site about only publishing trade figures we can verify and cite. For Africa, our verified per-country data currently covers fewer markets in the depth we'd like — Kenya is the clearest example, where we have a confirmed trade relationship in soybean meal but haven't yet verified precise country-level figures for other products.</p>
<p>That's a research gap, not a supply gap. If you're an African buyer and don't see your country listed with a dedicated guide, it doesn't mean we can't serve you — <a href="/get-a-quote/">send us your requirement</a> and we'll quote against it directly, being upfront about what we do and don't have verified data on.</p>

<h2>What to expect</h2>
<p>Transit to East African ports (Mombasa and similar) typically runs 10–18 days from Indian west-coast ports. Documentation follows the same principles as elsewhere: {{ site.complianceStatement | lower }}</p>
